findings.finding:
The model enforces exactly this:
- no quote character (schema);
- a split on single spaces;
- every ENROLLED word fully matches one of the 11 KERNEL_PARAMETERS patterns, and lockdown=integrity is present;
- MAINTENANCE is ENROLLED with MAINTENANCE_UNIT inserted exactly once, at any position.
Decision 2 states this exactly: 'neither name units nor carry unit-bearing values', and the maintenance entry is bound to the enrolled one. The K1 row and the model comment go further in four places. None of them weakens the K1 discriminator.
(a) 'no word of either entry names, adds, masks or overrides a systemd unit, carries root mount options'. In systemd v256:
- in a systemd initrd, root= makes fstab-generator write sysroot.mount (add_sysroot_mount). For a device path (passno 1) it also writes systemd-fsck-root.service (generator_write_fsck_deps, then write_fsck_sysroot_service). root= also disables gpt-auto root discovery;
- ro and rw are root mount flags: they set sysroot.mount's ro/rw option. On the host they suppress gpt-auto's remount-rw drop-in (add_partition_root_rw);
- the maintenance entry's own word names a unit.
systemd fixes these unit names, and no value names a unit, so the effect is benign. The sentence is still literally untrue.
(b) 'so both boot the same initramfs and root'. The binding fixes the initrd= words and the root= word only. Nothing on the command line names the kernel image an entry loads: the type #1 linux or efi key, or a UKI. So nothing binds:
- a built-in initramfs, which init/initramfs.c do_populate_rootfs unpacks before the external initrd;
- a UKI's embedded .initrd, and under Secure Boot its embedded .cmdline, which replaces the loader options (stub.c use_load_options);
- systemd-stub's sidecar cpios: /.extra/credentials, /.extra/global_credentials (from \loader\credentials), /.extra/sysext and /.extra/confext. If the initramfs ships systemd-sysext.service (ConditionDirectoryNotEmpty=|/.extra/sysext), it merges the sysext images in the initrd under the strict signed-image policy (sysext.c pick_image_policy). /.extra/confext is on the initrd confext search path (discover-image.c), but v256's systemd-confext.service has no condition on it.
Also, root=/dev/nfs is accepted (bound probe G10). It hands root selection to the initramfs, which may take the root from the network.
The K1 statement lists only 'the initramfs contents'. It names neither the kernel image or UKI of each entry nor these sidecars.
(c) Environment sentence. Two more allowlisted keys reach init's environment on some kernels: initrd= without CONFIG_BLK_DEV_INITRD (init/Makefile builds do_mounts_initrd.o only with it), and security= without CONFIG_SECURITY (security.o). Both are inert names for systemd. No allowlisted bare word reaches argv: ro and rw (do_mounts.c) and quiet (main.c) are always built.
(d) Credentials bullet. systemd-stub packs the /.extra credentials; systemd-boot v256 does not. import-creds also imports /.extra/global_credentials, into the encrypted, untrusted directory, so these credentials take effect only if they decrypt.

findings.requiredChange:
- Restate 'What this guarantees' in decision 2's terms:
  - no word names a unit or carries a unit-bearing value. root=, ro and rw only drive systemd's fixed root-mount units;
  - the two command lines differ only by the maintenance unit word, so both name the same initrd= files and root= device.
- Add to the K1 statement's W03/T04 list:
  - the kernel image or UKI that each entry loads (the type #1 linux or efi key), with its built-in initramfs, embedded .initrd and .cmdline, and addons;
  - systemd-stub's sidecars (/.extra/global_credentials, sysext and confext images);
  - optionally, a network root (root=/dev/nfs).
- Add initrd= and security= to the environment sentence, or write 'on kernels without the handler of an allowlisted key'.
- Apply the same wording to the model comment and the R5-1 disposition.

Restore one sentence in the K1 row or the K1 statement:
- on a non-confidential VM, systemd-boot (io.systemd.boot.kernel-cmdline-extra) and systemd-stub (io.systemd.stub.kernel-cmdline-extra) append SMBIOS words to /proc/cmdline;
- those words are part of both declared entries and must be inside the grammar.

findings.finding:
(a) The binding makes every one-sided command-line negative a two-defect case. V38, V47, V48, V49, V52, V53, V57, V59-V66, V69, V70 and V73-V76 also break 'maintenance boot entry differs from the enrolled entry beyond the maintenance unit'. V54, V58 and V68 are refused by the schema first.
The README names only L14, V15-V17 and V42 as second-defect cases.
I disabled the enrolled grammar, the maintenance grammar and the lockdown rule together. No case is accepted; only 20 refusal messages change to the binding message. So the vectors pin the grammar and the lockdown requirement only by their messages. An implementation checked on accept or refuse alone could omit both.
Under the binding, the two grammar rules imply each other. In round 5 these cases were single-defect: each was accepted with only the grammar rule disabled.
(b) Only V71 (a root= change) and V72 (an initrd= change) pin the binding. Every vector still passes under each of these weaker or different bindings: order-insensitive, set-based, superset, root=/initrd= words only, and 'unit last'.
Word order matters:
- the kernel (root_dev_setup) and fstab-generator each keep the last root=;
- the kernel loads microcode only from the first initrd.
No vector has a maintenance entry that differs only by a grammar word (probes GM3 drop lockdown=integrity, GM5 drop ro, GM6 add quiet) or only by word order (GM7). The model refuses all four.
(c) R5-4 residuals. No case changes under any of these hand mutants:
- re-admitting rootfstype=, which the README states is refused;
- any initrd= value. The class excludes ',', which stops early_initrd from treating initrd=<addr>,<size> as a physical range that reserve_initrd_mem uses;
- security=[a-z]+. security=apparmor disables SELinux as a legacy major LSM (ordered_lsm_parse), so the claim 'SELinux ... can only be enabled' rests on this pattern;
- lockdown=[a-z]+ and loglevel=[0-9]+;
- dropping root=/dev/ or LABEL=, or admitting any root= value (root=fstab, tmpfs, gpt-auto).

